import{_ as t,d as n,e as a,x as i}from"./chunks/framework.BRr8VP3a.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position","description":"","frontmatter":{"title":"D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position","xkChars":0,"xkWords":446},"headers":[],"relativePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-02.md","filePath":"en/theory/appendix-d/d-02.md"}'),s={name:"en/theory/appendix-d/d-02.md"};function o(r,e,c,d,l,p){return n(),a("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[i('<div class="theory-source"><h1 id="section-d-02" tabindex="-1"><span class="xk-num"><span class="xk-num__n">D.02</span><span class="xk-num__gap"></span></span>Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position <a class="header-anchor" href="#section-d-02" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;D.02 Knowledge, Concepts, Context, and Epistemic Position {#section-d-02}&quot;">​</a></h1><p>In the main text, see §2.1–2.7, §4.8, and §16.6 and 16.15. Research entries cover 02.01–02.08.</p><p>Experience, reasoning, practice, embodied experience, interpretation of texts, and normative argument carry distinct burdens of proof. A formal derivation must disclose its premises and rules. An empirical judgment must show how observations support the claim. Interpretive research must consider the text, context, and competing readings together. Normative argument must state the reasons and positions affected. These forms of inquiry can constrain one another. They cannot all be compressed into one measure of predictive performance.</p><p>Falsification, paradigms, and research programs offer criticism at different scales. A local counterexample may refute an auxiliary hypothesis or require changing the object or measurement. Whether it undermines a broader theory depends on the original claim’s scope and dependencies. A different question can yield valuable new research, but its results cannot be recorded retroactively as success in the old test. Usefulness, actionability, consensus, and truth are distinct standards.</p><p>Language describes and can also constitute action. Making a promise, naming something, granting eligibility, and reporting a fact require different positions from which to speak, rules, and evidence. Someone saying “I consent” may be expressing their wishes sincerely, yet under particular pressures this may be insufficient for valid authorization. Understanding what they mean cannot substitute for checking the institutional conditions. A mismatch in language also cannot serve directly to diagnose personality or intelligence.</p><p>Epistemic position affects who can access material, what questions can be asked, and whose statements are more readily believed. Better conditions for hearing people, translating, and speaking safely can increase available knowledge. Statements by people who are excluded should still be assessed claim by claim. Exclusion neither disqualifies a statement in advance nor grants it an automatic claim to truth. Non-disclosure for protection can coexist with credible verification.</p><p>Methods can correct one another while retaining genuinely different theoretical commitments. The influence of position on knowledge production does not imply that objects can be made up at will. Reasons can enter a causal explanation without removing the separate task of understanding meaning. A reliable record of dates can correct a date someone remembers while preserving their standing to recount the experience and what it meant to them. Recognizing the speaker’s standing does not make every factual proposition automatically true.</p><p>Research topics. 02.01 Empiricism and rationalism (Hume, Descartes, and others). 02.02 Falsification, paradigms, and research programs (Popper, Kuhn, and Lakatos). 02.03 Pragmatism (Peirce, James, and Dewey). 02.04 Realism, constructivism, and critical realism. 02.05 Phenomenology (Husserl, Heidegger, and Merleau-Ponty). 02.06 Hermeneutics (Dilthey, Gadamer, and Ricœur). 02.07 Language, concepts, and context (Wittgenstein, Austin, and others). 02.08 Epistemic injustice and situated knowledge.</p></div>',1)])])}const h=t(s,[["render",o]]);export{u as __pageData,h as default};
